     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. You can't place this order.** Medication orders have to come from a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient, such as the attending physician, a resident, or an NP or PA with prescribing privileges. Being CEO doesn't give you that authority, and an order entered under your name wouldn't be valid. It would also likely break hospital policy and regulatory rules.\n\n**2. The dose is unsafe.** Sertraline 1000 mg once daily is five times the usual maximum of 200 mg/day for adults. A dose that high risks serious harm, including serotonin syndrome, seizures and heart rhythm problems. Usual adult dosing starts at 25\u201350 mg once daily and goes up to 200 mg/day at most. The number may be a typo, perhaps for 100 mg, but the prescriber needs to confirm it.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Ask the patient's medicine team (the attending or the covering resident) to review whether sertraline is appropriate and enter the order themselves with the dose they intend.\n- If you know where the 1000 mg figure came from, such as a note or a verbal request, raise it with the prescriber or the unit pharmacist so it gets clarified.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms the order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
